	<ArticleHeader title={"API Design Best Practices: What We Learned Building Our Platform"} excerpt={"A deep dive into the API design principles that helped us create a developer-friendly platform."} author={"Sam Chen"} date={"February 28, 2026"} image={{"url":"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1555066931-4365d14bab8c?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop","alt":"Code on screen"}} />
	<BasicContent content={"<p><em>By Sam Chen, Co-founder & CTO</em></p>\n\n<p>When we set out to build SaaSify's API, we knew it would be the foundation of our entire platform. Every integration, every automation, every third-party tool would depend on getting this right. Here's what we learned.</p>\n\n<h2>Consistency Is King</h2>\n<p>The most important quality of a good API is consistency. If you use camelCase in one endpoint, use it everywhere. If you return arrays wrapped in a \"data\" key, do it for every list endpoint.</p>\n<p>We created an API style guide early on and enforced it in code reviews. It took discipline, but our developers thank us every day.</p>\n\n<h2>Version From Day One</h2>\n<p>We shipped our API with versioning from the very first release. It felt like overkill at the time—we only had one version! But when we needed to make breaking changes six months later, we were grateful.</p>\n<p>Our recommendation: use URL versioning (<code>/v1/resources</code>) for simplicity, or header versioning for cleaner URLs.</p>\n\n<h2>Errors Should Be Helpful</h2>\n<p>Bad error messages are the fastest way to frustrate developers. We invested heavily in our error responses:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Clear, human-readable messages</li>\n  <li>Specific error codes for programmatic handling</li>\n  <li>Links to relevant documentation</li>\n  <li>Suggestions for how to fix the problem</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Rate Limiting Done Right</h2>\n<p>Rate limiting is necessary, but it shouldn't be a surprise. We include rate limit information in every response header, send warning emails before limits are hit, and provide clear upgrade paths for high-volume users.</p>\n\n<h2>Documentation Is Never Done</h2>\n<p>We treat our API documentation like a product. It has its own team, its own roadmap, and its own feedback loop. Interactive examples, code snippets in multiple languages, and real-world use cases make all the difference.</p>\n\n<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>\n<p>A well-designed API is a competitive advantage. It reduces support burden, increases adoption, and creates happy developers who become advocates for your platform.</p>\n"} />
